    "evidence_text": "Robustly Leveraging Prior Knowledge in Text Classification\tPrior knowledge has been shown very useful to address many natural language processing tasks.\n\nIntroduction\tWe posses a wealth of prior knowledge about many natural language processing tasks.\n\nA key problem arisen here, is how to leverage such knowledge to guide the learning process, an interesting problem for both NLP and machine learning communities.\n\nFirst, to leverage prior knowledge to label data  ,  .\n\nSecond, to encode prior knowledge with a prior on parameters, which can be commonly seen\n\nThird, to formalise prior knowledge with additional variables and dependencies  .\n\nOr, which kind of knowledge is appropriate for the task?\n\nand performs well when we provide prior knowledge with no bias.\n\nRelated Work\tThere have been much work that incorporate prior knowledge into learning, and two related lines are surveyed here.\n\nChen et al.chen2013leveraging tried to learn comprehensible topic models by leveraging multi-domain knowledge.\n\nThere are also some active learning methods trying to use prior knowledge.\n\nConclusion and Discussions\tThis paper investigates into the problem of how to leverage prior knowledge robustly in learning models.\n\nTo the best of our knowledge, this is the first work to address the robustness problem of leveraging knowledge, and may inspire other research.\n\nThe KL",
